From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 01:44:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: query the wireless driver for the
 flow TID

Packets that the PPE forwards to a WLAN station bypass the wireless
stack, so the SCS and MSCS rules of the station are not applied to
them. Query the wireless driver for the user priority of a flow before
the entry is bound, and store the result in the TID field of the WINFO
data. Only netsys v3 has this field.

The query happens in __mtk_ppe_check_skb(), because that is the only
place where the flow of an entry is known for both rule based entries
and the learned subflows of a bridge entry. The flow description is
built from the FOE entry and only includes the fields that the entry
matches on, since one answer has to be valid for every packet that hits
the entry. This leaves out the DSCP, which varies per packet, and the
ports of 3-tuple entries. If the driver cannot answer with the fields
provided, the entry stays unbound.

The DSCP of the packet that binds the entry is passed along for the
QoS map fallback, so that a flow without a matching rule gets the
priority that the software path gives its frames, instead of TID 0.

Add a flush callback for the wireless driver to use when the rules of
a station change, or when the learned priority of one flow changes. It
unbinds the matching entries of that station, so that the next packet
binds them again with a fresh query. A bridge entry has no hardware
entry of its own, so its subflows are removed instead and learned
again. The wireless driver selects the entries, since matching a flow
key needs cfg80211.

An AP learns the priority of an MSCS flow from its uplink frames, which
an offloaded uplink keeps from the wireless stack. A flush of a station
therefore also unbinds the flows that come from it. An entry records
the station when the WED receive path binds it. The flow key of the
wireless driver describes the frames to the station, so the match
callback learns whether an entry carries the frames of the station. For
such an entry the flow description takes the tuple before NAT, as the
station sent it. A routed entry holds the MAC addresses of the next hop,
so its description leaves them out.

The query runs in the receive path, where hw_lock cannot be taken.
Publish the WED device for it through a separate RCU protected pointer,
which is cleared and synchronised before the device is detached.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>

